<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  Fast DDS transport profile for large point clouds.

  A VLP-16 revolution at 0.2 degrees is 28800 points; at the 26-byte
  Livox layout Ultra-Fusion reads, one PointCloud2 is about 630 KB. Fast
  DDS sizes its UDP socket buffer from net.core.rmem_default, which is
  208 KB on a stock kernel: smaller than a single message. The fragments
  overflow the socket and the message is never reassembled.

  This does not fail loudly. The publisher reports success, nothing logs
  an error, and the only symptom is the subscriber quietly receiving a
  fraction of the messages. It cost a diverged Ultra-Fusion run to find:
  uf_link published 599 clouds while uf_node's own log reported
  arrivals{lidar=78}, an 87% loss, which presents as the estimator
  drifting 47% of distance rather than as a network problem. With this
  profile the same run receives 578 of 599 and drifts 0.17%.

  The buffers are 7 MB, sized to sit just under this machine's
  net.core.rmem_max of 7.5 MB. That ceiling is the real constraint:
  asking for more does NOT get more, because the kernel refuses the
  request and Fast DDS falls back to a smaller buffer without saying so.
  Raising net.core.rmem_max (root) is the only way past it.

  4 MB was enough for one robot and overran at four, which appears as
  "sequence size exceeds remaining buffer" flooding the subscriber and
  three of four estimators starved. Buffer size alone does not solve
  that: the durable fix is not to send the channel nobody reads, via
  the medium's "channels" attribute. A VLP-16 revolution is ~630 KB and
  four unread copies per 100 ms is the bulk of the traffic in a
  visual-only run.

  Applied by exporting FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE before launching
  anything, which run_uf.sh does for both uf_link and every uf_node.

  Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so
  keep prose here free of it.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>large_udp</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
        <sendBufferSize>7000000</sendBufferSize>
        <receiveBufferSize>7000000</receiveBufferSize>
        <!-- Stay under the 65535 byte UDP datagram limit; one cloud is
             fragmented across many of these. -->
        <maxMessageSize>65000</maxMessageSize>
        <maxInitialPeersRange>10</maxInitialPeersRange>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="large_data_participant" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>large_udp</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- Replace the builtin transports rather than adding to them,
             so nothing falls back to the default sized sockets. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
  </profiles>
</dds>
